  "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y=\\frac{{X}^{4}}{4}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that $Y\\in {0,\\frac{1}{4},4}.$ Notice that we now have two columns for $Y=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWe calculate the probabilities associated with each value of $Y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=0) & =0.3 \\\\ P(Y=\\frac{1}{4}) & =0.2+0.4 \\\\ P(Y=4) & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following table.\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, $b=4,c=0.6,$ and $b+c=4.6.$",
